        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "861ef6a2-a6cf-5337-a771-76f8db6eda80",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Bolivar, Ohio; Glasgow, Pennsylvania; Lisbon, Ohio; Los Angeles, California; Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains; Beaver Creek State Park\n- Projects: Sandy and Beaver Canal; Los Angeles Aqueduct; Second Los Angeles Aqueduct\n- Organizations: Los Angeles Department of Water and Power; American Society of Civil Engineers\n- Systems: Los Angeles Aqueduct system\n- Events: Cold Run Reservoir Dam failure; historic landmark recognition\n\nFacts:\n- The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 miles from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\n- The Sandy and Beaver Canal ceased operating in 1852 after the Cold Run Reservoir Dam outside Lisbon, Ohio, broke and ruined a large portion of the canal.\n- The Los Angeles Aqueduct system comprises the Los Angeles Aqueduct, also called the Owens Valley aqueduct, and the Second Los Angeles Aqueduct.\n- The Los Angeles Department of Water and Power built and operates the Los Angeles Aqueduct system.\n- The Los Angeles Aqueduct system delivers water from the Owens River in the Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains to Los Angeles, California.\n- In 1971, the American Society of Civil Engineers recognized the Los Angeles Aqueduct system on its List of Historic Civil Engineering Landmarks.\n- Beaver Creek State Park contains historic remnants of the Sandy and Beaver Canal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "bd2b23da-0471-55c3-ad7c-81cab1de879e",
        "text": "Page: Sandy and Beaver Canal. Sentence: The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 mi from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\nPage: Sandy and Beaver Canal. Sentence: The canal ceased to operate in 1852, when the Cold Run Reservoir Dam outside of Lisbon, Ohio, broke, ruining a large portion of the canal.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: The Los Angeles Aqueduct system, comprising the Los Angeles Aqueduct (Owens Valley aqueduct) and the Second Los Angeles Aqueduct, is a water conveyance system, built and operated by the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: The system delivers water from the Owens River in the Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains to Los Angeles, California.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: In 1971 it was recognized by the American Society of Civil Engineers on the List of Historic Civil Engineering Landmarks.\nPage: Beaver Creek State Park. Sentence: Historic remnants of the Sandy and Beaver Canal can be found throughout the park.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
